1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:13,000
Think you know sticky stuff?

2
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:14,000
Well think again.

3
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:20,000
Sure it's an office staple, but can it really build a skyscraper?

4
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:23,000
Rip it, smear it, stretch it.

5
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:27,000
Just don't step in it, cause this stuff burns.

6
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:30,380
From the roads we drive to the air we breathe,

7
00:00:31,140 --> 00:00:32,640
everything's coming up sticky.

8
00:00:33,300 --> 00:00:34,340
So stick around.

9
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:38,280
It's Sticky Stuff on Modern Marvels.

10
00:00:49,560 --> 00:00:51,800
Before there was man or history,

11
00:00:54,860 --> 00:00:56,520
there was sticky stuff.

12
00:00:57,000 --> 00:00:59,000
It seeped and oozed.

13
00:00:59,001 --> 00:01:02,000
It even nourished.

14
00:01:02,001 --> 00:01:08,000
40,000 years after Neanderthals first used tar to affix points to spears,

15
00:01:08,001 --> 00:01:14,000
scientists and engineers still look to solve the riddle of why stuff sticks

16
00:01:14,001 --> 00:01:19,000
and then apply those answers to everyday problems.

17
00:01:19,001 --> 00:01:22,000
Scientifically, things stick for lots of reasons,

18
00:01:22,001 --> 00:01:26,440
But few things have proven as effective as a tape that many

19
00:01:26,441 --> 00:01:28,780
consider the strongest in the world.

20
00:01:28,781 --> 00:01:34,420
It's called VHB tape, created by the 3M company.

21
00:01:34,421 --> 00:01:35,980
This is our slammer demo.

22
00:01:35,981 --> 00:01:38,280
And what we're going to do here is we're going to impact a 16

23
00:01:38,281 --> 00:01:41,080
pound bowling ball into two aluminum sheets that have

24
00:01:41,081 --> 00:01:43,580
been bonded together with a VHB tape.

25
00:01:43,581 --> 00:01:45,660
And we'll see how long the VHB tape lasts.

26
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:58,180
You can see that it did eventually fail, but it did take a lot of impacts.

27
00:01:59,500 --> 00:02:03,880
In fact, that's 10 times stronger than rivets, using the same test.

28
00:02:10,240 --> 00:02:16,000
Now you can see, after just two impacts, how the mechanical fasteners pulled through the aluminum.

29
00:02:16,001 --> 00:02:22,000
Every yard of VHB represents 3M's veteran know-how when it

30
00:02:22,001 --> 00:02:23,000
comes to tape.

31
00:02:23,001 --> 00:02:26,000
They've been in the business for over eight decades.

32
00:02:26,001 --> 00:02:29,000
We've been making tapes and adhesives here for about 33

33
00:02:29,001 --> 00:02:33,000
years, and we make today over 12,000 different tapes and

34
00:02:33,001 --> 00:02:35,000
adhesives every year.

35
00:02:35,001 --> 00:02:39,000
But one of our stickiest tapes is VHB tape.

36
00:02:39,001 --> 00:02:43,000
Used in everything from planes to home electronics

37
00:02:43,001 --> 00:02:45,000
electronics, and everywhere in between.

38
00:02:47,740 --> 00:02:49,880
VHB is tape on steroids.

39
00:02:51,480 --> 00:02:55,040
Fast, easy to apply, and as this test shows,

40
00:02:55,640 --> 00:02:57,160
able to pull a train car.

41
00:03:00,080 --> 00:03:03,400
The secret behind VHB tape is acrylic foam,

42
00:03:05,840 --> 00:03:08,540
which makes it airtight, watertight,

43
00:03:08,900 --> 00:03:10,700
and highly viscoelastic.

44
00:03:10,701 --> 00:03:15,240
Viscoelasticity is one of the most powerful ways things stick.

45
00:03:17,700 --> 00:03:24,100
When first applied, the VHB tape seeps and flows into the nooks and crannies of the surface to which it will adhere.

46
00:03:26,140 --> 00:03:27,740
This is the tape's viscosity.

47
00:03:28,980 --> 00:03:34,900
And it will continue to seep for up to 72 hours after initial application to achieve maximum stick.

48
00:03:34,901 --> 00:03:43,120
Now, as that material is flowing, if you start to pull on it, now the material starts acting

49
00:03:43,121 --> 00:03:46,780
like a solid, instead of acting like a liquid.

50
00:03:46,781 --> 00:03:52,420
So the way it forms a bond is through viscous flow, and the way it forms a strong bond is

51
00:03:52,421 --> 00:03:56,280
by acting like a solid under the application of stress.

52
00:03:56,281 --> 00:04:03,240
Here's a little demonstration of the elasticity of the VHB tape.

53
00:04:03,241 --> 00:04:06,480
So you can see you can elongate it quite a bit,

54
00:04:06,481 --> 00:04:09,180
and it still has resiliency to go back,

55
00:04:10,080 --> 00:04:12,840
and we can still take it right back again

56
00:04:12,841 --> 00:04:14,840
to show strength and resiliency.

57
00:04:16,360 --> 00:04:18,580
VHB tape's exact chemical formula

58
00:04:18,581 --> 00:04:22,700
resides inside this jar, but sorry, that's classified.

59
00:04:23,860 --> 00:04:25,360
So if we can't say what's in it,

60
00:04:26,840 --> 00:04:30,140
we can show its most spectacular use.

61
00:04:30,141 --> 00:04:32,960
The facades that are used in making a number

62
00:04:32,961 --> 00:04:38,120
modern skyscrapers are actually made out of panels that are just decorative in character.

63
00:04:38,560 --> 00:04:43,220
And those decorative panels are very often constructed using an acrylic foam tape like

64
00:04:43,221 --> 00:04:48,580
VHB tape in order to attach the panel to a frame, and then that frame is attached to

65
00:04:48,581 --> 00:04:49,281
the building.

66
00:04:50,760 --> 00:04:55,320
But even decorative additions need to be strong and durable, especially in extreme

67
00:04:55,321 --> 00:04:56,021
environments.

68
00:04:56,500 --> 00:05:02,240
One of the most famous buildings that utilizes VHB tape is a Bajour-El Arab Hotel, which

69
00:05:02,241 --> 00:05:04,620
is the tallest hotel in the world.

70
00:05:04,621 --> 00:05:08,500
Also, it's a great example of an extremely harsh environment

71
00:05:08,501 --> 00:05:10,920
with high temperatures and high wind loads,

72
00:05:10,921 --> 00:05:13,560
which proves the durability and the ultimate performance

73
00:05:13,561 --> 00:05:16,400
of the VHB tape.

74
00:05:16,401 --> 00:05:19,000
To meet intense environmental conditions,

75
00:05:19,001 --> 00:05:22,340
3M has done extensive heat tests to compare VHB tape

76
00:05:22,341 --> 00:05:23,041
to rivets.

77
00:05:26,320 --> 00:05:28,180
And the hotel's engineers chose it

78
00:05:28,181 --> 00:05:30,080
to connect the composite aluminum panels

79
00:05:30,081 --> 00:05:31,320
to the 60-story frame.

80
00:05:35,020 --> 00:05:37,760
Soaring heat, for example, causes the aluminum panels

81
00:05:37,761 --> 00:05:40,620
to expand, creating pressure on the connection.

82
00:05:43,540 --> 00:05:47,400
Unlike rivets, the viscoelasticity of VHB foam

83
00:05:47,401 --> 00:05:50,200
absorbs the stress and allows the aluminum panel

84
00:05:50,201 --> 00:05:52,800
to move independently of the steel to which it is stuck.

85
00:05:52,801 --> 00:06:02,080
Similarly, VHB tape bolsters the reinforcements that secure the dramatic curves of the Disney

86
00:06:02,081 --> 00:06:06,480
Concert Hall in Los Angeles against intense winds and seismic activity.

87
00:06:09,480 --> 00:06:16,940
While VHB may be the Superman of tape products, the power of viscoelasticity may one day yield

88
00:06:16,941 --> 00:06:19,000
all kinds of superheroes.

89
00:06:20,700 --> 00:06:22,240
This is free-running.

90
00:06:22,800 --> 00:06:31,740
A new extreme sport made possible through the sticky power of a cutting-edge rubber on the soles of the athlete's shoes.

91
00:06:32,860 --> 00:06:34,440
It's called stealth rubber.

92
00:06:35,640 --> 00:06:44,380
And while not quite at Spider-Man levels yet, this new material can give any athlete a real step up.

93
00:06:44,381 --> 00:06:53,100
Although more commonly known for its bounce than its adhesive qualities, rubber stickiness

94
00:06:53,101 --> 00:06:58,820
allows dragsters to catapult and motorcycles to corner.

95
00:06:58,821 --> 00:07:04,940
In 1985, Charles Cole combined his interests in rock climbing and chemistry to formulate

96
00:07:04,941 --> 00:07:08,340
stealth rubber, the stickiest of them all.

97
00:07:08,341 --> 00:07:13,820
Our whole job here is making better rubber, but when I say making, I mean inventing.

98
00:07:13,821 --> 00:07:15,180
how modern marbles get done.

99
00:07:15,260 --> 00:07:16,300
We do it in a lab.

100
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:19,920
Historically, rubber was a natural product.

101
00:07:20,600 --> 00:07:23,460
But today, nearly all rubber is synthesized.

102
00:07:24,960 --> 00:07:28,280
So we're going to begin with the chunks of polymer.

103
00:07:28,820 --> 00:07:31,940
First thing added, we just drop them down the chute.

104
00:07:32,760 --> 00:07:34,800
Now we're going to start putting in the different powders.

105
00:07:35,460 --> 00:07:36,700
It's the secret powders.

106
00:07:40,120 --> 00:07:43,240
Stealth rubber's stickiness depends on its viscoelasticity.

107
00:07:43,820 --> 00:07:46,520
The more viscous, the stickier.

108
00:07:46,920 --> 00:07:47,880
So we've dumped the rubber.

109
00:07:48,240 --> 00:07:49,880
This is what it looks like when it comes out.

110
00:07:50,080 --> 00:07:52,100
All the ingredients are not yet in it.

111
00:07:52,600 --> 00:07:53,940
All right, so now we're going to put it on the mill,

112
00:07:54,080 --> 00:07:55,420
which will help cool it down.

113
00:07:55,740 --> 00:07:58,020
At the same time, we're going to be mixing ingredients.

114
00:07:59,900 --> 00:08:02,780
But the exact balance between visco and elastic

115
00:08:02,781 --> 00:08:04,560
is a closely guarded recipe.

116
00:08:05,380 --> 00:08:06,860
That's in a safe deposit box.

117
00:08:06,920 --> 00:08:07,620
That's in my head.

118
00:08:07,621 --> 00:08:08,620
That's in my computer.

119
00:08:09,100 --> 00:08:11,160
And my mom knows the secrets, too.

120
00:08:13,820 --> 00:08:16,240
Uncured rubber breaks down in a matter of days.

121
00:08:17,980 --> 00:08:20,200
By adding sulfur-based curing agents,

122
00:08:20,760 --> 00:08:23,920
Cole ensures that his rubber soles can withstand constant pounding,

123
00:08:24,840 --> 00:08:25,980
giving them their elasticity.

124
00:08:26,760 --> 00:08:28,520
And now we're going to take it off.

125
00:08:29,880 --> 00:08:31,400
We're going to cut the rubber off.

126
00:08:35,620 --> 00:08:38,100
So we just pull it off the mill after cutting it.

127
00:08:38,101 --> 00:08:47,780
And we're going to repeat this operation several times to make sure everything is mixed in.

128
00:08:53,300 --> 00:08:54,205
The result?

129
00:08:54,206 --> 00:09:02,600
A highly viscous, sticky rubber used by today's elite climbers to take on the most extreme challenges.

130
00:09:03,540 --> 00:09:06,400
There's essentially two ways you can use your climbing shoe while you're climbing.

131
00:09:06,401 --> 00:09:11,220
One is for a technique called edging, and that's where you use the sharp edge of the shoe,

132
00:09:11,840 --> 00:09:15,520
and you put it on something very small, and even though it's a tiny foothold,

133
00:09:16,460 --> 00:09:20,040
the stealth rubber allows you to stand up on that and hold yourself up on the wall.

134
00:09:21,440 --> 00:09:23,200
Smearing is the other way you can use a climbing shoe,

135
00:09:23,340 --> 00:09:27,500
and that's where you have essentially a flat wall with no relief in it,

136
00:09:27,840 --> 00:09:30,060
so you would just put your foot straight on the wall,

137
00:09:30,280 --> 00:09:34,660
and just the pressure against the wall allows you to hold on with the stealth rubber.

138
00:09:36,400 --> 00:09:39,900
Like VHB tape, stealth rubber's secret is its ability

139
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:43,200
to interlock with irregularities in the rock surface,

140
00:09:43,300 --> 00:09:45,400
even when they're not clearly visible.

141
00:09:45,500 --> 00:09:49,240
We're looking for anything, and it can be down at the real fine

142
00:09:49,340 --> 00:09:51,240
microscopic level, the irregularities,

143
00:09:51,340 --> 00:09:53,560
or the large particles.

144
00:09:53,660 --> 00:09:56,460
Different rocks have different surfaces.

145
00:09:56,560 --> 00:09:59,560
At some point, they all have irregularities.

146
00:09:59,660 --> 00:10:01,960
There's nothing that's truly flat.

147
00:10:01,961 --> 00:10:10,340
as the shoe comes in contact with the stone surface friction activates the viscosity of

148
00:10:10,341 --> 00:10:16,360
the shoe's rubber causing it to flow into the rocks irregularities creating a small but powerful

149
00:10:16,361 --> 00:10:22,960
interlock meanwhile the rubber's elasticity guarantees that this interlock is solid

150
00:10:22,961 --> 00:10:29,280
and supports the climber's weight as he or she ascends upward with the stealth rubber you're

151
00:10:29,281 --> 00:10:32,120
guaranteed to hook onto it.

152
00:10:32,121 --> 00:10:33,980
You're guaranteed to stick to it.

153
00:10:33,981 --> 00:10:37,420
And then with that, you'll be able to actually stand up

154
00:10:37,421 --> 00:10:40,860
on that hold and support your whole body weight on something

155
00:10:40,861 --> 00:10:45,440
that you can barely see in the rock.

156
00:10:45,441 --> 00:10:47,960
Together, the interlocked shoe and rock wall

157
00:10:47,961 --> 00:10:52,660
form a kind of momentary stair, thanks to Cole's lab.

158
00:10:52,661 --> 00:10:54,900
With the stealth rubber, it will actually

159
00:10:54,901 --> 00:10:56,980
flow into the cracks.

160
00:10:56,981 --> 00:10:59,000
The rubber actually has to almost break,

161
00:10:59,001 --> 00:11:03,240
or in certain cases has to break for you to actually fall off the rock.

162
00:11:06,880 --> 00:11:09,880
Remarkably, stealth rubber works just as well underwater.

163
00:11:10,080 --> 00:11:11,440
It doesn't matter if it's wet or not.

164
00:11:12,260 --> 00:11:14,700
As long as the penetration, the interlock is deep enough,

165
00:11:15,440 --> 00:11:17,060
and then it cannot come out.

166
00:11:17,540 --> 00:11:19,940
It doesn't matter. The liquid is independent of interlock.

167
00:11:22,100 --> 00:11:25,460
Such versatility has created a growing market for this sticky stuff.

168
00:11:25,461 --> 00:11:30,460
The company I met yesterday wanted it for chefs in kitchens

169
00:11:30,461 --> 00:11:33,460
on the greasy floors and that kind of thing.

170
00:11:33,461 --> 00:11:36,460
But when I'm thinking about this, I'm thinking construction.

171
00:11:36,461 --> 00:11:40,460
If I was 800 feet up on an I-beam walking across it,

172
00:11:40,461 --> 00:11:43,460
I'd really want my rubber on my shoes.

173
00:11:45,460 --> 00:11:49,460
But while some sticky stuff is born in a lab...

174
00:11:49,461 --> 00:11:50,460
It's not coming off.

175
00:11:50,461 --> 00:11:51,320
It's not coming off.

176
00:11:54,560 --> 00:11:58,740
It's the secrets of nature that have inspired remarkable discoveries.

177
00:11:59,800 --> 00:12:06,100
From everyday products like Velcro fasteners to the greatest climber of all.

178
00:12:12,540 --> 00:12:17,140
They appear as alien life forms when seen under intense magnification.

179
00:12:17,141 --> 00:12:24,460
On one side, a dense forest of tiny loops, nearly 8,000 per square inch.

180
00:12:25,240 --> 00:12:28,300
On the other, a lesser number of microscopic hooks.

181
00:12:29,500 --> 00:12:33,640
When pressed together, they become entangled in a sticky mechanical bond.

182
00:12:35,460 --> 00:12:42,260
Hook and loop, simple, ingenious, and the secret behind the fasteners made by the company Velcro USA.

183
00:12:42,261 --> 00:12:50,400
The name Velcro comes from the French words velour and crochet, which stand for hook and loop.

184
00:12:53,180 --> 00:12:59,700
Today, Velcro USA manufactures more than 250 million yards of their sticky product every year.

185
00:13:01,020 --> 00:13:06,760
Yet for all their simplicity of use, Velcro brand fasteners are deceptively complex to manufacture.

186
00:13:06,761 --> 00:13:15,140
When you buy a sneaker with a hook and loop closure on it, you can't imagine all the

187
00:13:15,141 --> 00:13:18,540
ways that that material's been looked at and tested before you get it.

188
00:13:18,541 --> 00:13:19,540
We'll burn it.

189
00:13:19,541 --> 00:13:21,140
We'll weigh it.

190
00:13:21,141 --> 00:13:25,580
We'll put adhesive on the back of it and see how that adhesive performs in an oven and

191
00:13:25,581 --> 00:13:27,040
in a freezer.

192
00:13:27,041 --> 00:13:33,440
We do infrared spectroscopy, whether it's a blood pressure cuff or a diaper.

193
00:13:33,441 --> 00:13:35,440
When you think about it, it's pretty amazing.

194
00:13:35,441 --> 00:13:42,500
The manufacture of Velcro products begins with two kinds of commercial grade synthetic

195
00:13:42,501 --> 00:13:43,201
nylon.

196
00:13:43,202 --> 00:13:50,740
Monofilament, used as single strands to create the hooks, and multifilament, braided

197
00:13:50,741 --> 00:13:53,197
strands that form the loops.

198
00:13:53,198 --> 00:13:58,200
Unlike nearly all other sticky stuff, Velcro products are

199
00:13:58,201 --> 00:14:03,000
created on high-powered looms that weave the filament at a rate of 900 strokes per minute.

200
00:14:03,001 --> 00:14:07,000
It's like a horizontal sewing machine.

201
00:14:07,001 --> 00:14:13,000
There's a needle that goes across, ties a knot, comes back, just like a sewing machine

202
00:14:13,001 --> 00:14:16,000
goes down through the fabric, ties a knot, and comes back up.

203
00:14:16,001 --> 00:14:18,000
So now it's horizontally.

204
00:14:18,001 --> 00:14:24,000
This loom does about 5,000 yards a week.

205
00:14:24,001 --> 00:14:29,000
Coming off the looms, all material has been woven into loops, and only later will the

206
00:14:29,001 --> 00:14:34,460
Later, will the monofilament undergo a separate process to become hooks.

207
00:14:34,461 --> 00:14:40,720
Before that can occur, the material is placed inside these vats, where it is cleaned, dyed

208
00:14:40,721 --> 00:14:46,820
for color, and then heat set, a crucial step in the process, especially when it comes to

209
00:14:46,821 --> 00:14:51,040
the hooks.

210
00:14:51,041 --> 00:14:56,960
All plastic has what engineers call memory, which means that even if woven into a loop,

211
00:14:56,961 --> 00:15:01,298
will return to its original straight form unless acted upon.

212
00:15:01,299 --> 00:15:03,900
So just as a curling iron reshapes a

213
00:15:03,901 --> 00:15:09,760
woman's hair, heat is applied to break the filament's memory, locking it into a curled position.

214
00:15:11,460 --> 00:15:17,700
Now, fine shears cut the monofilament at a fixed angle, creating a small barb on every end,

215
00:15:18,360 --> 00:15:19,063
the hook.

216
00:15:19,064 --> 00:15:25,000
The tolerances to make that cut on a very small monofilament are very tight.

217
00:15:25,001 --> 00:15:30,680
If it's cut too high or too low or not at all, you don't get the mating of the hook and the loop.

218
00:15:31,780 --> 00:15:37,120
And the company went through several years of experimentation and testing in order to perfect that process.

219
00:15:38,380 --> 00:15:42,280
Once cut, the material is married together, rolled, and prepped for shipping.

220
00:15:44,700 --> 00:15:50,900
Remarkably, the inspiration for hook and loop technology didn't arise in a lab, at least not at first,

221
00:15:51,320 --> 00:15:53,760
but rather during a simple walk in the woods.

222
00:15:55,000 --> 00:16:01,500
The idea for a hook and loop fastener came from a Swiss engineer by the name of George

223
00:16:01,501 --> 00:16:03,620
De Maestro.

224
00:16:03,621 --> 00:16:08,360
George De Maestro was walking through the woods and he noticed the cockleburrs which

225
00:16:08,361 --> 00:16:10,500
were sticking to his clothes.

226
00:16:10,501 --> 00:16:16,100
And he, being an innovative type of person, he decided he would look into a microscope

227
00:16:16,101 --> 00:16:19,600
and see what made the cockleburrs stick.

228
00:16:19,601 --> 00:16:24,400
And he realized that they were tiny hooks and these hooks were what would attach to

229
00:16:24,401 --> 00:16:27,660
his wool trousers or onto his dog's fur.

230
00:16:28,380 --> 00:16:32,860
He decided that this was an idea for a possible fastener.

231
00:16:35,960 --> 00:16:38,620
DeMistral's insight is today hailed as the forerunner

232
00:16:38,621 --> 00:16:40,860
of the emerging field of biomimetics,

233
00:16:41,440 --> 00:16:44,820
where nature's secrets provide inspiration

234
00:16:44,821 --> 00:16:47,360
to both biologists and engineers alike.

235
00:16:47,361 --> 00:16:55,200
and it's leading to some cutting-edge sticky stuff.

236
00:16:56,740 --> 00:17:00,220
Call her StickyBot, a first-generation robot

237
00:17:00,221 --> 00:17:02,140
able to do something truly amazing.

238
00:17:03,280 --> 00:17:04,400
She climbs.

239
00:17:10,240 --> 00:17:12,700
There are robots already being used,

240
00:17:12,880 --> 00:17:14,720
for example, by the military in Iraq.

241
00:17:14,840 --> 00:17:16,160
There's something called PacBot

