00:33:24,420 --> 00:33:27,560
And the electrostatic precipitator charges the particles

482
00:33:27,561 --> 00:33:29,580
so they're attracted to collecting plates.

483
00:33:30,720 --> 00:33:34,840
In the United States, more than 6,000 electrostatic precipitators

484
00:33:34,841 --> 00:33:39,260
prevent millions of tons of fly ash, acids, and micro-debris

485
00:33:39,261 --> 00:33:40,900
from entering the air each year.

486
00:33:43,980 --> 00:33:46,080
Particles carried in the factory's emissions

487
00:33:46,081 --> 00:33:50,300
enter a nexus of steel plates and ominous-looking electrode wires

488
00:33:50,301 --> 00:33:52,860
hanging 30 to 50 feet above the ground.

489
00:33:53,880 --> 00:33:56,300
Modern precipitators are more built with these,

490
00:33:56,640 --> 00:33:58,500
what they call rigid discharge electrodes,

491
00:33:58,680 --> 00:34:01,140
which is just a simple pipe section

492
00:34:01,141 --> 00:34:05,480
that's going to be, in this case, just a little demo unit,

493
00:34:05,600 --> 00:34:07,900
but will be about 30 to 40 feet tall

494
00:34:07,901 --> 00:34:11,420
and built with these little spikes sticking up.

495
00:34:13,180 --> 00:34:16,960
Up to 80,000 volts of electricity flow through these electrodes,

496
00:34:17,300 --> 00:34:20,240
which causes them to throw off electrons into space.

497
00:34:20,300 --> 00:34:23,600
an effect known as a corona.

498
00:34:24,700 --> 00:34:27,520
These electrons attach themselves to the passing particles,

499
00:34:28,180 --> 00:34:31,360
creating in them a negatively charged electrostatic imbalance.

500
00:34:32,480 --> 00:34:37,740
You've got a negative charged particle near a negatively charged electrode,

501
00:34:38,180 --> 00:34:43,740
and your basic fifth grade science class says two like charges repel from each other,

502
00:34:43,900 --> 00:34:45,240
so it moves away.

503
00:34:45,241 --> 00:34:53,000
Now, it moves away and is deflected away from the discharge electrodes towards a collecting plate.

504
00:34:54,460 --> 00:35:01,720
Once deflected, the particles seek out a positive and attract to the collection plates like cling wrap to a bowl.

505
00:35:03,080 --> 00:35:08,480
As the material collects onto the plate, typically it'll build a layer of material.

506
00:35:08,481 --> 00:35:18,580
And we'll allow the material to build up between an eighth and upwards of a half an inch, typically, before we wrap it.

507
00:35:19,540 --> 00:35:25,440
After said intervals, small but powerful metal hammers slam the top of each collection plate,

508
00:35:26,100 --> 00:35:29,800
causing the particles to fall into large hoppers, ready for disposal.

509
00:35:30,460 --> 00:35:33,260
The process itself is about as simple as gravity.

510
00:35:33,261 --> 00:35:38,180
The complexity is in making it as efficient as possible.

511
00:35:38,480 --> 00:35:44,040
Today, that same electrostatic technology has been adapted into ionizing air filters

512
00:35:44,041 --> 00:35:48,660
for the home, making static electricity more useful than ever.

513
00:35:48,661 --> 00:35:56,720
But while you might get a charge out of electrostatics, consider another sticky generator, the bees.

514
00:35:56,721 --> 00:36:01,480
They're making more than honey in those hives, and it's creating quite a buzz.

515
00:36:01,481 --> 00:36:06,480
The modern city, hectic, fast-paced, industrious.

516
00:36:07,480 --> 00:36:09,480
But think we're busy?

517
00:36:09,481 --> 00:36:11,480
Consider the bees.

